#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Kodachi Dock Control - re-arm the dock's trash monitor
#
# SPDX-License-Identifier: LicenseRef-Kodachi-SAN-1.1
# Copyright (c) 2013-2026 Warith Al Maawali
#
# This file is part of Kodachi OS.
# For full license terms, see LICENSE.md or visit:
# https://kodachi.cloud/docs/license.html
#
# Commercial or organizational use requires a written license.
# Contact: warith@digi77.com
#
"""Re-arm the dock's recycle-bin monitor when its gvfs backend has been replaced.

THE DEFECT THIS CLOSES. cairo-dock's ``dustbin`` applet owns no trash code. At
start it asks the desktop-environment backend (xfce-integration, gvfs-based) for
the trash path, gets the URI ``trash://``, and puts ONE GIO directory monitor on
it. That monitor is served by the gvfs backend process ``gvfsd-trash`` over a
private unix socket. ``gvfsd-trash`` lives in gvfs-daemon.service's cgroup, so
every restart or kill of gvfsd takes the backend with it, the monitor dies with
the socket, and the applet neither notices nor retries: it has no reconnect path,
its 10 s polling fallback is armed ONLY when the monitor could not be created at
start, and it logs nothing. The icon then stays on whichever image it last drew,
however much lands in the trash. Measured on the operator's PC on 2026-09-13:
gvfsd was restarted at 17:34 and 18:47, the dock kept a socket whose peer inode
read 0, and 34G moved into the trash at 19:26 left the icon on the empty
wastebasket. Reproduced in isolation three times under a private session bus
(Kodachi-Dock-Playbook § 43.1).

THE RE-ARM. cairo-dock's own D-Bus interface, org.cairodock.CairoDock, offers
``Reload(query)``. ``Reload("module=dustbin")`` stops and restarts the applet,
which creates a fresh monitor through the live gvfsd; a new monitor in the same
process recovers even after BOTH gvfsd and gvfsd-trash were replaced (probe 3 of
the same day). No dock restart, no visible effect beyond the icon redrawing.

THE CHECK, which is what decides. A live monitor is a unix socket in the dock
whose peer belongs to a ``gvfsd-trash`` process. ``ss -xp`` shows both ends for
the calling user's own processes without privilege, so linked-or-not is one read
of that table. The helper acts only when the dock is running, is older than a few
seconds (a fresh dock is still arming itself), ss attributed at least one socket
to the dock (a blind table must never be read as "unlinked"), and no dock socket
peers a gvfsd-trash socket.

THIS RUNS ONCE A MINUTE FOR THE LIFE OF A LOGIN, SO EVERY WAY OF FAILING MUST BE
BOUNDED. Two inspector rounds were spent on exactly this and the second one found
the half the first fix missed, so the rule is stated once here and applied to
EVERY outcome below, not only to the interesting one:

    no tick may take an action, write a journal record, or return non-zero more
    than MAX_ATTEMPTS times in a row for one dock before the helper goes quiet.

  1. THE APPLET MAY LEGITIMATELY NOT BE THERE. ``dustbin`` sits in ``modules=`` by
     default, but that file is per-user and cairo-dock rewrites it, so a user who
     removes the Trash cell has no dustbin instance and therefore no trash socket
     FOREVER. Before reloading, ask the dock whether the applet exists:
     ``GetProperties("module=dustbin")`` returns a populated array for a live
     applet and an EMPTY array for a module that is not instantiated (measured
     against `clock` and `weather`, which are not in this theme's ``modules=``).
  2. A RE-ARM THAT NEVER TAKES MUST STOP TRYING. Consecutive failures are counted
     in a state file under $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR keyed by the dock's pid; after
     MAX_ATTEMPTS the helper backs off for QUIET_S and says so. A new dock pid, or
     one success, clears the state.
  3. A BROKEN INSTALL COUNTS TOO, AND THIS IS THE HALF THE FIRST FIX MISSED. If
     ``ss`` cannot run, python3-gi is absent, the dock's bus name is gone, or
     ``Reload`` itself times out, the helper cannot repair anything, so retrying
     every 60 s buys nothing and costs a failed unit plus two journal records per
     minute, for the life of the login, with a ``Reload`` still being delivered in
     the call-timeout case. Every one of those paths now goes through
     ``bounded_failure()`` and is capped by the same counter.
  4. A COUNTER THAT CANNOT BE PERSISTED CANNOT BOUND ANYTHING, so the helper does
     not act at all when its state file is unwritable: it says so once and returns
     0 rather than reloading forever with a counter that resets every tick.
  5. THE BACKOFF GATE RUNS BEFORE ``ss``. It needs only the /proc walk, so a
     backing-off tick spawns no subprocess and writes nothing.

THE TIME BUDGET. ``WORST_CASE_S`` is the sum of the steps that carry an EXPLICIT
bound: two ``ss`` runs at SS_TIMEOUT_S and two D-Bus calls at DBUS_TIMEOUT_MS,
plus the settle. It is NOT the whole wall clock: ``Gio.bus_get_sync`` takes no
timeout (it connects to a local unix socket, so it is bounded in practice but not
by us) and the /proc walks are unbounded in principle. That is a reason the unit's
TimeoutStartSec is double WORST_CASE_S rather than a hair above it, and
test_the_unit_budget_exceeds_the_helpers_own_worst_case enforces the margin while
test_the_helper_makes_only_the_blocking_calls_the_budget_assumes counts the calls
so a third one cannot be added silently.

Exit status: 0 for every ordinary outcome, including a re-arm that did not take
once the helper has given up on this dock, "the applet is not there", and any
capped failure after the cap is reached. 1 while a re-arm is still being retried.
2 for a broken install (``ss`` unusable, python3-gi missing, the bus name
unreachable) while it is still being retried. Both 1 and 2 stop once the cap is
reached, so neither can outlive MAX_ATTEMPTS ticks for one dock.
"""

import json
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys
import time

DOCK_COMM = "cairo-dock"
BACKEND_COMM = "gvfsd-trash"
BUS_NAME = "org.cairodock.CairoDock"
OBJECT_PATH = "/org/cairodock/CairoDock"
APPLET_QUERY = "module=dustbin"
RELOAD_QUERY = APPLET_QUERY

# A dock younger than this is still creating its own monitor; leave it alone.
MIN_DOCK_AGE_S = 20.0
# How long a reload gets to mount trash:// and connect before the re-check.
SETTLE_S = 5.0
# The two calls that can block on something outside this process.
SS_TIMEOUT_S = 10.0
DBUS_TIMEOUT_MS = 10000
# The sum of the EXPLICITLY bounded steps only: ss twice (before and after), two
# D-Bus calls (GetProperties then Reload), and the settle between them. See the
# time-budget paragraph in the module docstring for what this deliberately does
# NOT cover.
WORST_CASE_S = 2 * SS_TIMEOUT_S + 2 * (DBUS_TIMEOUT_MS / 1000.0) + SETTLE_S
# How many blocking calls of each kind that sum assumes, asserted by the suite so
# a third one cannot be added without the budget moving with it.
EXPECTED_SS_CALLS = 2
EXPECTED_DBUS_CALLS = 2

# Consecutive failed ticks for one dock pid before backing off. Applies to every
# failure mode, not only to a re-arm that did not take.
MAX_ATTEMPTS = 3
# How long to stay quiet after giving up, or after finding no dustbin applet.
QUIET_S = 3600.0
STATE_BASENAME = "kodachi-dock-trash-rearm.state"

# One `ss -xp` row for a connected unix socket. The local column is `*` for an
# unnamed client socket and a filesystem path for a named server socket, and the
# gvfsd-trash side is the NAMED one, so a parser that expects `*` on both sides
# drops exactly the rows this helper exists to find (that mistake was made and
# measured while writing it). The peer column is always `*`: verified against
# this machine's live table, 539 of 539 rows carrying `users:(`.
SS_ROW = re.compile(
    r"^u_\w+\s+\S+\s+\d+\s+\d+\s+(?P<local>\S+)\s+(?P<lino>\d+)\s+\*\s+(?P<pino>\d+)"
    r"\s+users:\((?P<users>.*)\)\s*$")
PROC_REF = re.compile(r'\("(?P<comm>[^"]+)",pid=(?P<pid>\d+),fd=(?P<fd>\d+)\)')


class Broken(Exception):
    """A broken install rather than a state this helper is meant to repair."""


# ── output ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# THE HEALTHY PATH PRINTS NOTHING, AND A LOG LEVEL WILL NOT DO IT FOR YOU. This
# runs once a minute for the life of every graphical session, so an unconditional
# status line is a persistent journal record a minute, about 1440 a day per
# session, for a check whose answer is almost always "nothing to do".
#
# The first attempt at this emitted routine status with a `<7>` debug prefix and
# relied on the unit's LogLevelMax=notice to drop it. MEASURED on systemd 257
# (Debian 13), user unit, one tick, cursor-bounded:
#
#     LogLevelMax=debug    11 journal lines  (systemd's own About to execute /
#                                             Forked / Starting / Executing /
#                                             Finished, plus our line)
#     LogLevelMax=notice    1 journal line   (ours, at PRIORITY=7)
#
# So LogLevelMax does real work, ten lines of it, on the messages SYSTEMD
# generates about the unit, and it does NOT drop a `<N>`-prefixed line the
# service writes to stdout: journald stores that line at the prefixed priority
# regardless. A debug prefix is therefore not a filter, and the only reliable way
# to write nothing is to write nothing. The unit keeps LogLevelMax=notice for the
# ten lines it does suppress.
#
# Everything that DOES reach the journal is an event worth finding later, so
# emit() tags it notice. The prefix goes out only when systemd is capturing our
# output (it sets JOURNAL_STREAM), so an interactive `--check` prints clean text.

def _under_systemd():
    return "JOURNAL_STREAM" in os.environ


def emit(message):
    sys.stdout.write(("<5>" if _under_systemd() else "") + message + "\n")
    sys.stdout.flush()


# ── observation ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def own_pids(comm):
    """PIDs of this user's processes whose comm is exactly `comm`, ASCENDING.

    Sorted because the first element keys the retry state. `os.listdir("/proc")`
    order is not defined, so with two cairo-dock processes for one user an
    unsorted list can flip between ticks, which would make the state key flip,
    reset the counter every time and defeat the cap.
    """
    uid = os.getuid()
    found = []
    for name in os.listdir("/proc"):
        if not name.isdigit():
            continue
        try:
            if os.stat("/proc/" + name).st_uid != uid:
                continue
            with open("/proc/%s/comm" % name, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
                if fh.read().strip() != comm:
                    continue
        except OSError:
            continue
        found.append(int(name))
    return sorted(found)


def process_age_s(pid):
    """Seconds since the process started, from /proc/<pid>/stat field 22."""
    with open("/proc/%d/stat" % pid, encoding="utf-8", errors="replace") as fh:
        stat = fh.read()
    # comm can hold spaces and parentheses; every field after the last ')' is
    # fixed. state is field 3, starttime is field 22, so it is index 19 here.
    fields = stat[stat.rindex(")") + 2:].split()
    start_ticks = int(fields[19])
    with open("/proc/uptime", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
        uptime = float(fh.read().split()[0])
    return uptime - start_ticks / os.sysconf("SC_CLK_TCK")


def dock_processes():
    """(pids, ages) for this user's docks. Pure /proc: no subprocess, cannot raise
    Broken, so the backoff gate can run before anything expensive."""
    pids = own_pids(DOCK_COMM)
    ages = []
    for pid in pids:
        try:
            ages.append(process_age_s(pid))
        except (OSError, ValueError, IndexError):
            # Unreadable: treat as old and let the socket check decide, rather
            # than blocking a real re-arm on a /proc read.
            ages.append(MIN_DOCK_AGE_S)
    return pids, ages


def read_ss():
    try:
        done = subprocess.run(["ss", "-xp"], capture_output=True, text=True,
                              timeout=SS_TIMEOUT_S)
    except FileNotFoundError as exc:
        raise Broken("ss is not installed (iproute2): %s" % exc) from exc
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired as exc:
        raise Broken("ss did not finish within %.0fs" % SS_TIMEOUT_S) from exc
    if done.returncode != 0:
        raise Broken("ss exited %d: %s" % (done.returncode, done.stderr.strip()[:200]))
    return done.stdout


def parse_ss(text):
    """Connected unix sockets as {local, peer, procs:[(comm, pid, fd)]}."""
    rows = []
    for line in text.splitlines():
        match = SS_ROW.match(line)
        if not match:
            continue
        procs = [(p.group("comm"), int(p.group("pid")), int(p.group("fd")))
                 for p in PROC_REF.finditer(match.group("users"))]
        rows.append({"local": int(match.group("lino")),
                     "peer": int(match.group("pino")),
                     "procs": procs})
    return rows


def link_state(rows, dock_pids, backend_pids):
    """Is any dock socket connected to a gvfsd-trash socket?

    dock_rows counts sockets ss attributed to the dock at all (the dock always
    holds X11 and D-Bus sockets, so zero means ss could not see the dock, not
    that the dock has none). links lists (dock pid, dock fd, peer inode) pairs.
    """
    dock_pids = set(dock_pids)
    backend_pids = set(backend_pids)
    dock_rows = [r for r in rows if any(pid in dock_pids for _, pid, _ in r["procs"])]
    backend_locals = {r["local"] for r in rows if any(pid in backend_pids for _, pid, _ in r["procs"])}
    links = [(pid, fd, r["peer"]) for r in dock_rows for _, pid, fd in r["procs"]
             if pid in dock_pids and r["peer"] in backend_locals]
    return {"dock_rows": len(dock_rows), "backend_rows": len(backend_locals), "links": links}


def observe():
    """The full read: dock processes plus the socket table. Raises Broken."""
    dock_pids, ages = dock_processes()
    state = link_state(parse_ss(read_ss()), dock_pids, own_pids(BACKEND_COMM))
    return dock_pids, ages, state


# ── persisted retry state ────────────────────────────────────────────────

def state_path():
    """Where the retry counter lives.

    $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR is per-user, tmpfs, mode 0700, and cleared when the last
    session of that user ends, which is exactly the lifetime this counter should
    have. Every systemd user session sets it. The /tmp fallback exists only for a
    hand-run outside a session; it is uid-qualified so two users cannot collide,
    and load_state refuses a file that is not ours, because /tmp is world-writable
    and a pre-created foreign file could otherwise hand us an arbitrary
    quiet_until or make every write fail.
    """
    base = os.environ.get("XDG_RUNTIME_DIR")
    if base:
        return os.path.join(base, STATE_BASENAME)
    return "/tmp/%s.%d" % (STATE_BASENAME, os.getuid())


def load_state():
    clean = {"dock_pid": 0, "fails": 0, "quiet_until": 0.0}
    path = state_path()
    try:
        fd = os.open(path, os.O_RDONLY | os.O_NOFOLLOW)
    except OSError:
        return clean
    # THE fd MUST BE CLOSED ON EVERY PATH, and an early `return` inside the try
    # below does not do it: only the os.fdopen arm did, so the foreign-uid branch
    # and any fstat error leaked one descriptor per call (measured: 50 calls on
    # that branch leaked 50, against 0 on the owned-file control). Harmless in the
    # shipped process, which calls this once and exits, and wrong anyway.
    adopted = False
    try:
        if os.fstat(fd).st_uid != os.getuid():
            # Somebody else's file in a shared directory: never trust its numbers.
            return clean
        fh = os.fdopen(fd, encoding="utf-8")
        adopted = True                      # fdopen owns the fd from here
        with fh:
            data = json.load(fh)
        if not isinstance(data, dict):
            raise ValueError("not an object")
        return {"dock_pid": int(data.get("dock_pid", 0)),
                "fails": int(data.get("fails", 0)),
                "quiet_until": float(data.get("quiet_until", 0.0))}
    except (OSError, ValueError, TypeError, json.JSONDecodeError):
        # A missing or corrupt state file must never stop a re-arm: start clean.
        return clean
    finally:
        if not adopted:
            try:
                os.close(fd)
            except OSError:
                pass


def save_state(state):
    """Persist the counter. Returns True on success.

    THE RETURN VALUE IS LOAD-BEARING. A counter that cannot be written cannot
    bound anything: `fails` would be re-derived as 1 on every tick and the cap
    would never be reached, so a helper that ignored this would reload the applet
    and fail its unit every 60 s forever, which is the exact runaway the cap
    exists to prevent. Callers refuse to act when this returns False.
    """
    path = state_path()
    tmp = path + ".new"
    try:
        with open(tmp, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            json.dump(state, fh)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
        return True
    except OSError:
        try:
            os.unlink(tmp)
        except OSError:
            pass
        return False


def fresh_state(dock_pid):
    return {"dock_pid": dock_pid, "fails": 0, "quiet_until": 0.0}


def can_persist():
    """Can the counter be written? Answered WITHOUT rewriting the real state file.

    main() asks this on every tick that gets past the backoff check, so using
    save_state() here would rewrite the state file about 1440 times a day. The
    cost is nil on a tmpfs and the CONFUSION is not: a state file whose mtime is
    always under a minute old reads as "a failure was just recorded", which is
    exactly the wrong thing for the file a human inspects when the icon is stuck.
    A probe beside it answers the same question and leaves the real file's mtime
    meaning what it says.
    """
    probe = state_path() + ".probe"
    try:
        with open(probe, "w", encoding="utf-8") as fh:
            fh.write("{}")
        os.unlink(probe)
        return True
    except OSError:
        return False


def _state_writability():
    """One line for `--check`, because a timed tick handles an unwritable state
    directory SILENTLY (it must, or it reports the same thing every minute) and a
    human would otherwise have no way to see why nothing is happening."""
    path = state_path()
    if can_persist():
        return "%s is writable" % path
    return ("%s is NOT writable: every timed tick will do nothing and stay silent "
            "until this is fixed" % path)


def backoff_active(retry, dock_pids, now=None):
    """Is this dock inside a backoff window? Needs no subprocess, so it can gate
    the `ss` run rather than following it."""
    if retry is None or not dock_pids:
        return False
    if retry["dock_pid"] != dock_pids[0]:
        return False
    now = time.time() if now is None else now
    return now < retry["quiet_until"]


# ── decision ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def decide(dock_pids, dock_ages, state, retry=None, now=None):
    """Pure decision: ('skip' | 'reload', reason).

    `retry` is the persisted counter (None means "no cap", used by --check).
    """
    if not dock_pids:
        return "skip", "no cairo-dock process for this user, nothing to re-arm"
    young = [age for age in dock_ages if age < MIN_DOCK_AGE_S]
    if young:
        return "skip", "dock started %.0fs ago and is still arming itself" % min(young)
    if state["dock_rows"] == 0:
        return "skip", "ss attributed no socket to the dock, refusing to act on a blind reading"
    if state["links"]:
        pid, fd, peer = state["links"][0]
        return "skip", "trash monitor linked: dock %d fd %d peers gvfsd-trash socket %d" % (pid, fd, peer)
    if backoff_active(retry, dock_pids, now):
        now = time.time() if now is None else now
        return "skip", ("backing off for another %.0fs after %d unsuccessful attempt(s) "
                        "on dock %d" % (retry["quiet_until"] - now, retry["fails"], dock_pids[0]))
    return "reload", ("dock holds no socket to any gvfsd-trash process "
                      "(%d gvfsd-trash socket(s) visible): its trash monitor is dead" % state["backend_rows"])


def bounded_failure(retry, message, rc):
    """Report a failure ONCE PER ATTEMPT and stop after MAX_ATTEMPTS.

    EVERY FAILING EXIT OF A TIMED TICK goes through here: the re-arm that did not
    take, and the broken-install ones (ss unusable, python3-gi absent, the bus
    name gone, Reload timing out). The argument-parse `return 2` in main() does
    NOT, and does not need to: the unit passes no arguments, so nothing the timer
    runs can reach it. That sentence is written narrowly on purpose, because an
    absolute one here was itself a finding.

    THREE INSPECTOR ROUNDS SHAPED THIS, each finding the same runaway by a new
    route. Round one rejected an unbounded retry. The cap written to close it
    covered only "the re-arm did not take", so round two found three `return 2`
    branches still producing a failed unit and two journal records a minute.
    Round three found that the counter written HERE ignored whether it had
    actually been persisted: with an unwritable state directory `fails` was
    re-derived as 1 on every tick, the cap could never be reached, and the unit
    failed once a minute for the life of the login. main() now proves the state
    file is writable BEFORE anything that could repeat, and this function checks
    the return too, because a directory can become unwritable between the two.
    """
    if retry is None:                       # --check: report, never persist
        emit(message)
        return rc
    retry["fails"] += 1
    if retry["fails"] >= MAX_ATTEMPTS:
        retry["quiet_until"] = time.time() + QUIET_S
        if not save_state(retry):
            return _unpersistable(message)
        emit("%s; giving up after %d attempt(s) and staying quiet for %.0fs. The recycle-bin "
             "icon will not follow the trash until this is fixed or the dock is restarted: "
             "systemctl --user restart kodachi-dock.service" % (message, retry["fails"], QUIET_S))
        return 0
    if not save_state(retry):
        return _unpersistable(message)
    emit("%s (attempt %d of %d)" % (message, retry["fails"], MAX_ATTEMPTS))
    return rc


def _unpersistable(message):
    """The counter could not be written, so this tick must not report a failure it
    would repeat forever. Say what happened once, exit 0 so the unit is not left
    failed, and let main()'s writability gate keep every later tick silent until
    the directory is fixed."""
    emit("%s; and the retry counter could not be written to %s, so this will not be retried "
         "or reported again until that is fixed" % (message, state_path()))
    return 0


# ── the dock's D-Bus interface ───────────────────────────────────────────

def _bus():
    try:
        import gi
        gi.require_version("Gio", "2.0")
        gi.require_version("GLib", "2.0")
        from gi.repository import Gio, GLib
    except (ImportError, ValueError) as exc:
        raise Broken("python3-gi is missing or unusable (%s: %s)" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)) from exc
    try:
        # NOT covered by DBUS_TIMEOUT_MS, which applies to call_sync only. This
        # connects to a local unix socket, so it is bounded in practice and not
        # by us; it is one of the reasons the unit's budget is double
        # WORST_CASE_S rather than a hair above it.
        return Gio.bus_get_sync(Gio.BusType.SESSION, None), Gio, GLib
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001  (GLib.Error and friends)
        raise Broken("cannot reach the session bus (%s: %s)" % (type(exc).__name__, exc)) from exc


def _call(method, query):
    bus, Gio, GLib = _bus()
    try:
        return bus.call_sync(BUS_NAME, OBJECT_PATH, BUS_NAME, method,
                             GLib.Variant("(s)", (query,)), None,
                             Gio.DBusCallFlags.NONE, DBUS_TIMEOUT_MS, None)
    except Exception as exc:  # noqa: BLE001
        # SEPARATE DIAGNOSES, because one fixed message for every failure is the
        # defect class this project keeps finding: name the method and the real
        # exception rather than always blaming an absent bus name.
        raise Broken("%s(%r) on %s failed (%s: %s)" % (
            method, query, BUS_NAME, type(exc).__name__, exc)) from exc


def applet_is_present():
    """True when the dustbin applet is instantiated in the running dock.

    GetProperties returns one entry per matching icon/instance for a live applet
    and an EMPTY array for a module that is not loaded, so this distinguishes
    "the monitor died" from "the user removed the Trash cell".
    """
    reply = _call("GetProperties", APPLET_QUERY)
    (entries,) = reply.unpack()
    return bool(entries)


def reload_applet():
    _call("Reload", RELOAD_QUERY)


# ── main ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

def main(argv):
    check_only = "--check" in argv
    unknown = [a for a in argv if a != "--check"]
    if unknown:
        sys.stderr.write("usage: kodachi-dock-trash-rearm [--check]\n")
        return 2

    # THE CHEAP GATE FIRST. dock_processes() is a /proc walk with no subprocess
    # and no bus, so a backing-off tick and a no-dock tick cost that and nothing
    # else: no `ss`, no D-Bus, no journal record.
    dock_pids, ages = dock_processes()
    retry = None if check_only else load_state()
    if retry is not None and dock_pids and retry["dock_pid"] != dock_pids[0]:
        # A different dock: whatever we learned about the old one is void.
        retry = fresh_state(dock_pids[0])
    if not check_only and (not dock_pids or backoff_active(retry, dock_pids)):
        return 0

    # A COUNTER THAT CANNOT BE WRITTEN CANNOT BOUND ANYTHING, so prove the state
    # file is writable BEFORE the first step that could repeat. This gate used to
    # sit just before the Reload, which left the `ss`-failure path upstream of it:
    # with an unwritable $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and an unusable `ss`, `fails` was
    # re-derived as 1 every tick, the cap was never reached, and the unit failed
    # and wrote a record once a minute for the life of the login. That is the same
    # runaway two earlier rounds were about, reached by a third route, and it is
    # why this is the FIRST thing a timed tick does after deciding it might act.
    #
    # It PROBES rather than writing the real state, because this runs on every
    # tick: save_state() here rewrote the state file about 1440 times a day, so
    # its mtime was always under a minute old and read as "a failure was just
    # recorded" to anyone inspecting it. Silence is deliberate too: a broken
    # runtime directory is not this helper's to report once a minute, and
    # `--check` prints it for a human who looks.
    if not check_only and not can_persist():
        return 0

    try:
        dock_pids, ages, state = observe()
    except Broken as exc:
        if check_only:
            # --check REPORTS, it never fails: a broken install is a finding to
            # print, not an exit code, and the name of its contract says "always
            # exits zero".
            print("dock=%s: cannot measure the dock's sockets: %s"
                  % (",".join(str(p) for p in dock_pids) or "-", exc))
            print("state file: %s" % _state_writability())
            return 0
        return bounded_failure(retry, "cannot measure the dock's sockets: %s" % exc, 2)

    action, reason = decide(dock_pids, ages, state, retry)
    status = "dock=%s backend_sockets=%d dock_sockets=%d links=%d decision=%s: %s" % (
        ",".join(str(p) for p in dock_pids) or "-", state["backend_rows"],
        state["dock_rows"], len(state["links"]), action, reason)

    if check_only:
        print(status)
        # The one thing a timed tick handles silently and a human cannot see.
        print("state file: %s" % _state_writability())
        return 0
    if action != "reload":
        # SILENCE IS THE FIX, not a log level: see the emit() note above. A
        # healthy or backing-off tick writes nothing at all, so the journal cost
        # of this timer at rest is zero records rather than 1440 a day.
        return 0

    # SECOND LINE OF DEFENCE, not the first. The gate above already refused this
    # tick if the counter was unwritable; this covers a directory that filled or
    # went read-only in the milliseconds since, so the Reload still cannot go out
    # with a counter that would reset on the next tick.
    if not save_state(retry):
        emit("cannot persist the retry counter to %s, so this tick will not act: an unbounded "
             "retry is worse than a stale icon. Fix the runtime directory and it resumes by "
             "itself." % state_path())
        return 0

    emit(status)
    try:
        if not applet_is_present():
            retry["quiet_until"] = time.time() + QUIET_S
            save_state(retry)
            emit("no dustbin applet in this dock (the Trash cell is not in the layout): "
                 "nothing to re-arm, quiet for %.0fs" % QUIET_S)
            return 0
        reload_applet()
    except Broken as exc:
        return bounded_failure(retry, "re-arm FAILED: %s" % exc, 2)

    time.sleep(SETTLE_S)
    try:
        dock_pids, ages, state = observe()
    except Broken as exc:
        return bounded_failure(retry, "re-arm sent but the re-check could not run: %s" % exc, 2)

    if state["links"]:
        pid, fd, peer = state["links"][0]
        save_state(fresh_state(pid))
        emit("re-armed: dock %d fd %d now peers gvfsd-trash socket %d" % (pid, fd, peer))
        return 0

    return bounded_failure(
        retry, "re-arm attempted but the dock still holds no gvfsd-trash socket after %.0fs"
        % SETTLE_S, 1)


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
